// The round-off of the last Newton step of the shipped Kepler solve, bounded by FPTaylor
// (plans/17 Ф28.D).
//
//   timeout 300 fptaylor --log-base-dir <tmp> --tmp-base-dir <tmp> --opt-x-abs-tol 0
//     --opt-x-rel-tol 0 --opt-f-rel-tol 1e-2 --opt-f-abs-tol 0 kepler_roundoff.fptaylor
//
// The kernel, its operation order and its domain are kepler_roundoff.gappa's (Ф28.C):
// crates/spacelab-elements/src/keplerian.rs, `eccentric_from_mean`, one step from the iterate E:
//
//   (sin_e, cos_e) = libm::sincos(E);  f = (E - e sin_e) - M;  step = f / (1 - e cos_e);  E1 = E - step
//
// FPTaylor's branch and bound takes no constraints, and Gappa's hypothesis that the step starts near
// the root, |(E - e sin_e) - M| <= 1e-12, is one. It goes into the parametrisation instead: M is
// written as E - e sin E - phi with phi the exact residual Fx, a real in [-1.001e-12, 1.001e-12]
// (Gappa's |fc - Fx| <= 6.1e-16 widens the computed 1e-12 to it). Every (E, e, M) of Gappa's domain
// has such a phi, and M itself is an input, never rounded, so nothing in the code's arithmetic
// changes. The exact quantities are then Fx = phi, Stepx = phi / (1 - e cos E), E - Stepx.
//
// libm's sin and cos (musl kernels) are assumed within one ulp, as in Ф28.C. FPTaylor states that as
// rnd[64, ne, 2]: a relative error of at most 2^-52, which one ulp never exceeds (here up to twice
// Gappa's 2^-53 absolute for values in [1/2, 1)). Every `rnd64` is one IEEE double rounding to
// nearest-even; no FMA.
//
// Result: |fc - Fx| <= 6.130e-16 (Gappa 6.1e-16), |step - Stepx| <= 4.486e-14 (Gappa 6.1e-14) and
// |E1 - (E - Stepx)| <= 4.530e-14 (Gappa 6.14e-14, ratio 0.738). FPTaylor is the tighter here: its
// libm error is relative, so a small sin or cos carries a small error. Observed in
// roundoff_samples.mac: 1.13e-14.

Variables
  float64 E in [-0.001, 6.3],
  float64 e in [0, 0.99],
  real phi in [-1.001e-12, 1.001e-12]
;
Definitions
  sc = rnd[64, ne, 2](sin(E)),
  cc = rnd[64, ne, 2](cos(E)),
  M = (E - e * sin(E)) - phi,
  t1 = rnd64(E - rnd64(e * sc)),
  fc = rnd64(t1 - M),
  fp = rnd64(1 - rnd64(e * cc)),
  step = rnd64(fc / fp),
  E1 = rnd64(E - step)
;
Expressions
  kepler_f = fc,
  kepler_step = step,
  kepler_next = E1
;
